WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.760
Your cortex is only about twice as thick as a mouse's.

2
00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:11.260
Yet its surface is roughly a thousand times larger.

3
00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:12.940
That is the puzzle.

4
00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:18.500
Across mammals, thickness changes little, while area changes enormously.

5
00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.380
How does a growing brain make more area without a thicker wall?

6
00:00:24.133 --> 00:00:27.693
Pasko Rakic studied the fetal rhesus monkey.

7
00:00:28.733 --> 00:00:34.453
Neurons born at one spot climb the same radial glial fibre and stack into a column.

8
00:00:35.493 --> 00:00:39.073
In 1988 he proposed the radial unit hypothesis.

9
00:00:40.193 --> 00:00:43.833
Each patch of progenitors, one unit, makes one column.

10
00:00:44.473 --> 00:00:46.293
The number of units sets the area.

11
00:00:46.893 --> 00:00:49.473
The number of neurons in each unit sets the thickness.

12
00:00:50.417 --> 00:00:51.857
Here is the arithmetic.

13
00:00:52.997 --> 00:00:57.437
While founder cells divide symmetrically, the pool doubles each round.

14
00:00:58.457 --> 00:01:04.177
Once they switch to asymmetric division, each extra division adds only one neuron to

15
00:01:04.177 --> 00:01:10.977
a column. In a simple model, three more symmetric rounds give 2 × 2 × 2.

16
00:01:11.477 --> 00:01:13.477
That is eight times as many units.

17
00:01:13.477 --> 00:01:19.097
Three extra asymmetric divisions add just three neurons to each column.

18
00:01:19.617 --> 00:01:21.177
That is a few percent more.

19
00:01:21.967 --> 00:01:27.887
Rakic proposed that a delay of only a few cell cycles could account for a large

20
00:01:27.887 --> 00:01:30.627
increase in cortical area across primates.

21
00:01:31.847 --> 00:01:33.307
One test came from mice.

22
00:01:34.307 --> 00:01:39.527
Anjen Chenn and Christopher Walsh made transgenic mice whose progenitors carried a

23
00:01:39.527 --> 00:01:41.647
stabilized form of beta-catenin.

24
00:01:42.547 --> 00:01:45.307
More progenitors kept re-entering the cell cycle.

25
00:01:46.027 --> 00:01:50.967
The mice grew enlarged brains with greater surface area, and the smooth cortex

26
00:01:50.967 --> 00:01:52.187
buckled into folds.

27
00:01:53.117 --> 00:01:56.577
In primates, the ventricle is not the only address.

28
00:01:57.617 --> 00:02:03.537
In human fetal cortex, filmed in slices, outer radial glia sit far from the

29
00:02:03.537 --> 00:02:09.077
ventricle. They keep a long fibre to the brain surface but have no process touching

30
00:02:09.077 --> 00:02:12.797
the ventricle. They divide there and renew themselves.

31
00:02:13.917 --> 00:02:16.997
Their fibres diverge and fan the scaffold outward.

32
00:02:18.177 --> 00:02:21.957
These cells are abundant in primates and ferret, and rare in mouse.

33
00:02:22.677 --> 00:02:26.877
The marmoset has them too, yet its cortex is nearly smooth.

34
00:02:27.597 --> 00:02:29.117
So they do not guarantee folding.

35
00:02:30.117 --> 00:02:36.197
Three duplicated genes found in humans, not chimpanzees, have been tested.

36
00:02:38.477 --> 00:02:44.697
ARHGAP11B increased basal progenitors in mouse embryos, and enlarged the neocortex

37
00:02:44.697 --> 00:02:46.197
in marmoset fetuses.

38
00:02:47.517 --> 00:02:53.217
NOTCH2NL kept progenitors dividing in mouse, and deleting it made human organoid

39
00:02:53.217 --> 00:02:55.077
progenitors differentiate early.

40
00:02:55.077 --> 00:03:00.217
But none was tested by removing the gene from a developing human brain.

41
00:03:01.197 --> 00:03:03.877
None shows that one gene made the human cortex.

42
00:03:04.937 --> 00:03:08.737
Keep the rule. To gain area, change the founder phase.
